<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Merged on top of the generated gen/apple Info.plist at iOS build time
       (Tauri reads Info.ios.plist next to tauri.conf.json). Keep this the
       COMPLETE set of iOS additions (mobile-app schema §2.8): the ONLY usage
       description is location (when-in-use; no camera/mic/photos/contacts/
       tracking), no ATS exceptions (every provider is HTTPS), and deliberately
       NO UIFileSharingEnabled / LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace — the app
       sandbox holds data/api-keys.json in plaintext, so the Files app must not
       expose it; CSV import is picker-only by design. -->
  <key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
  <string>SnowRaven uses your location to center the map on your current position.</string>
  <!-- Standard HTTPS only — skips the export-compliance question on every
       TestFlight upload. -->
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <false/>
  <!-- UIScene adoption, required by the iOS 27 SDK (apple-platform-readiness).
       Without it the app INSTALLS AND THEN FAILS TO LAUNCH:
       "_UIApplicationEvaluateRuntimeIssueForNoSceneLifecycleAdoption ...
       UIScene life cycle is required for apps built with this SDK", measured on
       an iOS 27.0 simulator against exactly this file set minus this block.

       It is declared in THREE places and they must stay identical, which
       `frontend/src/lib/iosSceneManifest.test.ts` asserts: gen/apple/project.yml
       (the xcodegen input), gen/apple/snowraven_iOS/Info.plist (what the build
       reads), and here. This copy is the one that matters most and the reason
       this file is not merely a duplicate: it is the only one of the three
       OUTSIDE gen/, so it is the only one that survives a re-run of
       `tauri ios init`, which DECISIONS.md v0.5.68 records re-stamping Tauri's
       placeholders over gen/apple. Tauri merges this file over the generated
       plist on every `tauri ios build`, so the manifest heals itself there.

       UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes must be TRUE. tao 0.35.3's
       multiple_scenes_enabled() reads exactly this key, and a FALSE value does
       not crash -- it sends tao down its non-scene startup path while UIKit runs
       the app in scene mode, so the process lives, WebKit finishes the page, and
       the window attaches to no scene and the screen stays BLANK. Verify a
       change here with a screenshot, never a process check. The accepted side
       effect is that iPadOS now advertises the app as multi-window-capable.

       The delegate class name must match tao's registered class exactly. Do NOT
       replace this with the empty UISceneConfigurations dict in Tauri's docs:
       that form traps under the iOS 27 SDK too (tauri-apps/tauri #15719),
       because the iOS 27 validator reads the STATIC manifest and ignores tao's
       runtime registration.

       CORRECTED v1.0.31: tao 0.35.3 DOES implement
       application:configurationForConnectingSceneSession:options: (registered
       at view.rs:752 whenever multiple_scenes_enabled() reads true, which the
       key above makes true), contrary to what this file and its two siblings
       used to say. Its implementation handed UIKit a freed UISceneConfiguration
       and crashed RELEASE builds on launch (tauri-apps/tao#1244), which is why
       src-tauri/vendor/tao carries the one-line tao#1245 patch. This manifest
       was the trigger of that crash, not its cause, and is still correct. -->
  <key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
  <dict>
    <key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <true/>
    <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
    <dict>
      <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
      <array>
        <dict>
          <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
          <string>Default Configuration</string>
          <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
          <string>TaoSceneDelegate</string>
        </dict>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
